WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.019 --> 00:00:04.960
Speaker 1: Listen to today's voicemail&nbsp;
kind of bums me out. It's about Cuba,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.760
and we talk sometimes about the revolutionary&nbsp;
left, it's a sort of pseudo populism on the left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:18.560
which sometimes blinds people to authoritarianism&nbsp;
and the lack of progressivism and authoritarianism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:27.040
where they end up supporting these, you know,&nbsp;
often military uniform wearing faux populists.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:32.240
And I've been talking this week about how&nbsp;
the U.S. has some blame for the situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.280
in Cuba thanks to the hypocritical and&nbsp;
counterproductive U.S. embargo of Cuba.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.400
I want the embargo to be dropped. I don't want&nbsp;
American involvement in Cuban politics. And yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:49.120
I cannot, as a progressive, support something as&nbsp;
authoritarian as the Castro regime, which came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.640
to power in 1959, handed power from brother&nbsp;
to brother in 2008. That's not progressive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.760
That's just not progressive. And sadly, I did&nbsp;
get a voicemail. It's very long. I'll only play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.560
some of it from a revolutionary leftist who&nbsp;
doesn't like my perspective. Take a listen.&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.760
Speaker 4: I'm really disappointed&nbsp;
in you the way you're framing Cuba&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:17.440
politically. You are purposefully leaving out the&nbsp;
full context. Fidel Castro is not start out as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.280
dictator nor a dictator wannabe.
Speaker 1: So, first of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.120
didn't start out as a dictator, conceding&nbsp;
that that's eventually what he became.&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:31.840
Speaker 4: OK, he was a reaction to stuff. I&nbsp;
see the regime friendly to the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:38.480
corporate industrial oligarchy. Sure. The people&nbsp;
in Cuba uprose because they were sick and tired of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:45.360
being paid lousy wages. Sure, they didn't want to&nbsp;
be serfs for the American industrial machine. OK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:53.040
Fidel Castro, I believe he was educated in the&nbsp;
United States. And so he had a rule that one out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.240
on his fellow Cubans and he made sure that they&nbsp;
understood the true nature of what was going on.&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:06.320
Speaker 1: Listen, this is how faux populist&nbsp;
authoritarians work. The fact that Castro came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:14.080
power in a context where there was genuine real in&nbsp;
the streets. Dissatisfaction with Cuban leadership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:21.200
doesn't change the fact that this is how faux&nbsp;
populism works. It's the populist rhetoric.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:27.440
And next thing you know, the same guys in&nbsp;
power for forty nine years with a police state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:33.120
with complete and total control over every&nbsp;
aspect of the people and end of the economy, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:40.240
fact that Castro replaced something bad and that&nbsp;
those who were protesting in the 50s in Cuba did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:46.800
so genuinely and for good reasons, doesn't mean&nbsp;
that we praise Fidel Castro in power since 1959,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:53.760
until 2008, at which point he hands power to his&nbsp;
brother. It's not progressive. I can't support it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:59.120
And this is you know, it's a very similar&nbsp;
playbook to Venezuela because in the 50s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:06.000
there were reasons to protest. Doesn't mean we now&nbsp;
